{
	"version": 5,
	"rows": [
		{
			"id": "tJbhUedM6s8Fcsqri",
			"name": "Unusual Background (Latent Psychic Potential)",
			"reference": "B96",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Psychic ability is a random, rare mutation of the human genome that lets its bearer perceive and touch the immaterium (the warp). Sources disagree on exact frequency — Imperial administrative texts and Dark Heresy-line background both describe it as \"rare but not vanishingly so,\" on the order of a handful of latent potentials per hive-sized population, with usably strong psykers far rarer still. Treat any precise ratio as flavour, not census data.\n\nDesign: Required to take Psyker (Talent), Warp Empowerment, or any trait carrying the Warp Sorcery power modifier below. Unusual Background (B96) leaves its cost to the GM; 10 points reflects that the ability itself is not learned but grants access to an entire framework of further abilities, roughly the going rate other published settings use for \"you may buy from this power list at all.\"",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "tpnMMryNnBH1v9942",
			"name": "Warp Empowerment",
			"reference": "TSOR4",
			"local_notes": "A direct reskin of Sorcerous Empowerment (Thaumatology: Sorcery, TSOR4):\nthe psyker's raw, trained capacity to draw on and shape the warp, as\ndistinct from Psyker (Talent, TSOR5's Sorcery Talent analogue, below)\nwhich only sharpens related skills. Requires Unusual Background (Latent\nPsychic Potential). It is the prerequisite for every Warp Sorcery-tagged\nability in this file (see Witch-sight and each discipline below); the\nNavigator's Third Eye and the Pariah/Untouchable genes carry no Warp\nSorcery modifier and so need no Warp Empowerment.\n\nCap, cast per Thaumatology: Sorcery (TSOR): a psyker may only know a\npower whose full, modified point cost is no greater than the point cost\nof their own Warp Empowerment (10 + 10/level). A power bought at a\nhigher level (more dice, more range, etc.) costs proportionally more and\nmay outgrow a psyker's Empowerment even after they first learned it at a\ncheaper level — raising the level, and cost, of Warp Empowerment is how\na psyker \"grows into\" costlier or higher-level workings.\n\nImperial Assignment scale (Adeptus Astra Telepathica / Black Ships)\nmapped to Warp Empowerment level and the point cap that gives, worked\nout from this file's actual (Warp Sorcery -20% + Perils of the Warp)\nmodified costs. Attack powers are bought at lore levels (Smite and\nMolten Beam 12 levels at 162 points, Inferno 68, Crush 88, Fire Bolt\n42, Flame Breath 30, Assail 28), so only a strong psyker can hold one\nat full strength; a weaker one buys the same power at fewer levels\n(cost is per level: Smite and Molten Beam 13.5, Inferno 8.5, Crush 11,\nFlame Breath 6, Fire Bolt and Assail 3.5).\n\n  Grade      | Empowerment level | Cap (10+10/lvl) | What the cap reaches\n  Iota       | 1                  | 20              | every non-attack power costing 20 or less: Endurance (2), Iron Arm (4), Warp Speed (4), Forewarning (11), Psychic Scream (12), Heroic Feats of Strength (12), Sanctuary (13), Mind Probe (14), Foresight (18), Misfortune (18), Haemorrhage (19), Astrotelepathy (20). Attack powers only at reduced levels: Smite 1 level, Fire Bolt or Assail up to 5 levels, Crush 1 level\n  Zeta       | 1                  | 20              | as Iota; low Psyker Talent limits skill use, not what they can know\n  Eta        | 2                  | 30              | adds Mental Interrogation (21), Null Zone (23), Terrify (28), Assail at full (28), Flame Breath at full (30). Smite 2 levels, Fire Bolt 8 levels, Crush 2 levels\n  Theta      | 3                  | 40              | adds Scrier's Gaze (35), Dominate (35), Gate of Infinity (40): the full non-attack roster. Fire Bolt 11 levels, Crush 3 levels, Smite 2 levels\n  Epsilon    | 5                  | 60              | Fire Bolt at full (42) with Flame Breath (30) and Assail (28) at full (a typical Wyrdvane battlefield psyker); Smite, Molten Beam or Crush at 4-5 levels\n  Delta      | 16                 | 170             | Smite and Molten Beam at full 12 levels (162), Inferno at full (68), Crush at full (88): the whole Imperial attack roster (a typical Sanctioned Primaris Psyker)\n  Gamma      | 16                 | 170             | as Delta, better skills from a higher Psyker Talent\n  Beta       | 18                 | 190             | as Delta with headroom to push one attack to 14 levels (189)\n  Alpha      | 20                 | 210             | full roster with headroom for high-level workings, up to 15 levels of Smite or Molten Beam (203) (Astartes Librarians, Black Ship candidates)\n  Alpha-Plus | 25+ (GM only)      | 260+            | 19 levels of Smite (257) and beyond; Vortex of Doom (280 as usually bought) needs level 27, so it stays just out of reach here\n\nA character need not buy Warp Empowerment up to their grade's full\nceiling: templates in this project buy only the level their actually-\nknown powers need, and note the grade's theoretical ceiling separately.\n\nLore: The Iota-to-Alpha-Plus assignment scale is drawn from the Adeptus Astra Telepathica's own grading of psychic potential as depicted across Dark Heresy-line material and repeated in Codex/Imperial Armour background; Black Library uses the same grades in passing (e.g. Eisenhorn-cycle and Ciaphas Cain references to Alpha-class talents). \"Alpha-Plus\" specifically denotes the rarest, most dangerous talents the Black Ships process. Only the Alpha/Beta/Gamma end of that ordering is well attested outside the RPG lines; the full Iota-to-Theta ordering used in the table above is Dark Heresy-line material and is not confirmed by the fiction.\n\nDesign: Sorcerous Empowerment (TSOR4) costs 10 base + 10/level (level 1 = 20 points, each further level +10) in the GCS master Thaumatology data; reused verbatim here under a 40k-appropriate name, since the underlying mechanic — \"how much warp-power you can channel at all, gating which spells/powers you may know by their modified cost\" — is exactly what an Imperial Assignment grade represents. The level-to-grade mapping above is derived, not asserted: it walks every power in this file's actual modified cost (recomputed under the new Warp Sorcery -20% modifier, see below) and finds the cheapest Empowerment level whose cap clears it, so \"Iota can only cast minor powers at full strength\" and \"Alpha can hold every attack power at full lore strength with room to spare\" are literally true of the numbers, not just flavour text. Levels were raised well past the old 1-7 range because attack powers now cost 162 points at lore scale rather than a handful.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Talent",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 1,
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "td78_QRwGNJymhkJ6",
			"name": "Warp Sorcery (Power Modifier)",
			"local_notes": "Not purchased on its own. Apply \"Warp Sorcery, -20%\" as a modifier to every advantage built as a psychic ability in this file (Innate Attack, Affliction, Mind Control, Mind Probe, Mind Reading, Telekinesis-based traits, Terror, etc.), cast per Thaumatology: Sorcery (TSOR) — casting any such power costs 1 FP, exactly as a Sorcery spell does. It does not apply to the Blank/Pariah gene or the Navigator's third eye, which are inborn mutations, not channelled acts.\n\nLore: Every psychic act in the fiction shares three traits: it can be shut down by a null field (Culexus Assassins, Officio Assassinorum \"blanks,\" null rods, hexagrammic wards, a ship's Geller field extending into a room), it can be sensed by other warp-touched minds (daemons home in on strong psykers; astropaths and Inquisitors can feel a working; the Black Ships find latent talents this way), and using it always risks the warp answering back (see Perils of the Warp, below). Black Library depictions (Eisenhorn, Ravenor, Ahriman, the Horus Heresy Librarius material) and Dark Heresy/Rogue Trader's psychic-power rules agree on all three points.\n\nDesign: Sorcery (-15%, Thaumatology: Sorcery, TSOR) is Basic's own Mana Sensitive-weight power modifier for spells cast from the caster's own inner power rather than ambient mana: it fails against blanks/pariahs, null rods, hexagrammic and psychic wards, and inside a Geller field, as appropriate to the scene — the mechanical equivalent of Sorcery's own \"no mana, no spell\" clause re-read for the 40k setting's specific null sources. Stacked with Detectable, -5% (kept from this project's own original design): other warp-sensitive beings (psykers, daemons, the Astronomican-attuned) can sense the use, roughly the mirror of Basic's No Signature enhancement (+20%, B111) which buys detectability away entirely; here only a narrow audience can detect it, so removing that audience's free ability to sense you is worth a smaller -5%. Net: -20%, applied identically to every Warp Sorcery-tagged ability so their relative costs stay comparable. This replaces the project's earlier bespoke -15% \"Warp\" modifier now that the whole framework is rebuilt on Thaumatology: Sorcery's chassis; Perils of the Warp (below) still stacks on top as the setting-specific risk Sorcery itself doesn't model.",
			"tags": [
				"Reference",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 0
		},
		{
			"id": "tfOxB4Cq8HU0pbtyT",
			"name": "Perils of the Warp (Limitation)",
			"local_notes": "Not purchased on its own. Apply \"Perils of the Warp, -10%\" (or the Severe\nvariant, -20%, for rituals, Great Powers, and interstellar workings called\nout below) to every Warp-tagged advantage.\n\nWhenever a character uses an ability carrying this limitation, roll vs\nWill (modified by any Pushing, see below, and by GM-set warp conditions:\n-1 to -5 near a Chaos icon, inside a warp storm, on a ship whose Geller\nfield is failing, etc.). Success: the power works with no ill effect.\nFailure, consult the margin:\n  - Failed by 1-2 (Minor Perils): lose 1 FP; migraine or nosebleed; -1 to\n    the next IQ-based roll this scene.\n  - Failed by 3-5 (Moderate Perils): 1d damage, ignores DR (warp-shock);\n    the power still fizzles; anyone adjacent makes a Fright Check.\n  - Failed by 6-9 (Major Perils): 2d damage, ignores DR; the psyker is\n    stunned (roll vs HT each turn to recover); a minor manifestation\n    appears nearby (cold spot, whispering, flickering shadow) and\n    witnesses roll Fright Check at -2.\n  - Failed by 10+, or an unmodified 18: Catastrophic. A warp rift may\n    open, or a daemon attempts possession: Quick Contest of Will against\n    the daemon (GM sets 12-16 for a minor one) or the psyker is possessed,\n    killed, or reduced to a Chaos Spawn, GM's call. The triggering power\n    still goes off, at full or worse effect, entirely out of its user's\n    control.\n\nPushing: before rolling, a psyker may declare up to 3 pushes. Each push\ngrants +1 to the ability's effective level (an extra die, +1 to an\nAffliction, one more yard of Move, etc., GM's call for the specific power)\nbut applies a cumulative -2 to this Perils roll. Pushing represents\ndrawing more of the warp than the caster's mind and body can safely carry.\n\nLore: Calibrated against Dark Heresy's Psychic Phenomena/Perils of the Warp tables, the best worked example of \"using your gift can doom you,\" and against countless Black Library scenes of psykers burning out, exploding, or being possessed the instant they overreach (Ahriman's own Rubric; Quixos in Eisenhorn; any Chaos-touched sorcerer's Perils rolls in the Black Library Horus Heresy Librarius stories).\n\nDesign: Standard, -10%: comparable in total weight to Basic's Costs Fatigue (-5%, B112, a guaranteed minor cost) stacked with a low-frequency chance of a serious complication similar in spirit to Unreliable (-5% to -10%, B113) — bundled here as one flat roll-and-consult-a-table limitation instead of tracked as separate costs, since the system assumes a single check per activation rather than a metered resource. Severe, -20%: used on rituals, Great Powers, and interstellar workings (Gate of Infinity, Astrotelepathy, Vortex of Doom, Sanctic Daemonology's Banishment and Sanctuary, Librarius's Null Zone) where any serious failure can open a rift regardless of scale — double the standard weight, echoing how Basic's Always On climbs from -10% to -40% (B113) as an effect becomes harder to control.",
			"tags": [
				"Reference",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 0
		},
		{
			"id": "tx6XCYvZwcgAQ5l_j",
			"name": "Psyker",
			"reference": "TSOR5",
			"local_notes": "This project's Sorcery Talent (Thaumatology: Sorcery, TSOR5) analogue:\napplies as Sorcery Talent applies to a sorcerer's skills in TSOR, just\nto a 40k-specific list — Psyniscience, Meditation, Hidden Lore (the\nWarp), and the Will roll to resist Perils of the Warp (see above) — a\nhouse extension of Talent's normal \"add to listed skills\" rule,\nprecedented by Magery's and Sorcery Talent's own habit of aiding\nrelated rolls beyond pure spellcasting. Reaction bonus: +1/level from\nother sanctioned psykers and the Astra Telepathica.\n\nPsyker (Talent) and Warp Empowerment are separate purchases on separate\naxes, exactly as Sorcery Talent and Sorcerous Empowerment are in TSOR:\nthis trait only sharpens related skills and Perils resistance, while\nWarp Empowerment (above) is what actually gates which powers a psyker\nmay know. A high-Talent, low-Empowerment psyker is a sharp, well-drilled\nmind who simply hasn't learned much yet; the reverse is a raw prodigy\nwho is clumsy with the skills around their gift.\n\nImperial Assignment scale (Adeptus Astra Telepathica / Black Ships),\nweakest to strongest, mapped to Talent level. See Warp Empowerment above\nfor the matching Empowerment level/point-cap column, which is the real\ngate on ability selection:\n  Iota    — Talent 0 (latent; register but rarely take Psyker traits at all)\n  Zeta    — Talent 1\n  Eta     — Talent 1\n  Theta   — Talent 2\n  Epsilon — Talent 2  (typical Wyrdvane conscript)\n  Delta   — Talent 3  (typical Sanctioned Primaris Psyker)\n  Gamma   — Talent 4\n  Beta    — Talent 4  (many Astropaths)\n  Alpha   — Talent 5  (Astartes Librarians, Black Ship candidates)\n  Alpha-Plus — Talent 6+ (beyond-play tier: Primarchs,\n    the most powerful Inquisitors, the worst Chaos sorcerers)\n\nLore: The Iota-to-Alpha-Plus assignment scale is drawn from the Adeptus Astra Telepathica's own grading of psychic potential as depicted across Dark Heresy-line material and repeated in Codex/Imperial Armour background; Black Library uses the same grades in passing (e.g. Eisenhorn-cycle and Ciaphas Cain references to Alpha-class talents). \"Alpha-Plus\" specifically denotes the rarest, most dangerous talents the Black Ships process. Grade names beyond Alpha/Beta/Gamma are RPG-line material only.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting four things (three skills plus the Perils resistance roll) sits at Basic's \"Medium Talent\" rate of 10 points/level (B90, three-to-five-skill bracket) rather than 5 (one-two skills) or 15 (six-nine skills) — the same rate Sorcery Talent itself uses (TSOR5, 10/level). Capped at level 6 for player characters; higher grades are GM-reference only, per the framework's \"Beyond play\" tier.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Talent",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 1,
			"base_points": 0,
			"max_levels": 6
		},
		{
			"id": "tHR86AagYD-Wkeb40",
			"name": "Witch-sight",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The first, mildest sign of the gift: an unlearned, passive prickling sense of daemonic taint, psychic activity, and warp corruption — how unsanctioned psykers are so often found, and how veteran Inquisitors and Space Marines notice a place has gone wrong before anything visible happens. Widely depicted; no single canonical source, consistent across Black Library and Dark Heresy-line material alike.\n\nDesign: Built as Detect (Psychic Activity & Daemonic Taint), an uncommon but not vanishingly rare category, base 10 per Basic's Detect cost table (B47-48). No Perils of the Warp: it is involuntary background sense, not a deliberate act of channelling. Full modified cost: 10 x 0.8 = 8 points; needs only Warp Empowerment level 1 (cap 20) — even an Iota-grade latent can have it, matching how often unsanctioned psykers are found by exactly this unlearned sense.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mRB5mzPrV6NKgQ2ob",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				}
			],
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "Tg1NlCdB0H4Cdc11K",
			"name": "Unsanctioned Psyker",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Discovery of an unregistered psyker is, at best, summary transport to a Black Ship and Soul-binding or the Scholastia Psykana; at worst, on-the- spot execution by any Adepta Sororitas, Inquisitor, or panicking mob with a lasgun. This is one of the most consistent beats in Black Library's Imperium (Eisenhorn's witch-hunts; the fate of countless minor characters across the Gaunt's Ghosts and Ciaphas Cain series) and Dark Heresy's default assumption for any PC psyker who hasn't been Sanctioned.\n\nDesign: A single Secret (Basic B152) covers it: discovery is not rare (any strong emotional moment or unlucky witness can trigger it) and its consequences are severe and often fatal (summary execution by any Inquisitor, Sister or mob), so it takes the Possible Death tier, -30 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Social",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
