		{
			"id": "tnY4pJ-kP-Epa-5Fc",
			"name": "Vortex of Doom (Great Power)",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Librarius Great Power: a tearing vortex of raw warp-force that can gut a building or a company of tanks, at genuine risk to everything and everyone nearby, caster included. Kept as its own top-level entry (rather than nested inside Librarius) so it can be added, or left out, independently of a Librarian's two ordinary signature powers — it is GM-reference only, in the same \"Beyond play\" bracket the framework reserves for Primarchs and Greater Daemons, not meant for ordinary Librarian play.\n\nDesign: Innate Attack (Crushing, 5/die), 20d starting suggestion, with Explosion at the 16-yard tier (+200%, B106), Armor Divisor (2) (+50%), Preparation Required (-30%, a full turn's channelling), Warp Sorcery, and Severe Perils, net +180%. Full modified cost: 5 x 2.8 = 14 points/level, and this Great Power's minimum purchase is 20 levels (280 points) — deliberately priced in the framework's \"Beyond play\" tier. Even Alpha-Plus's GM-only Warp Empowerment ceiling (level 25, cap 260) falls short of 280 (it takes level 27): per the framework, Great Powers may exceed any PC's (or ordinary NPC's) Warp Empowerment outright. A GM allowing a named NPC to cast it at all should treat the cap rule as waived for this entry specifically, exactly as Thaumatology: Sorcery's own greater rituals routinely exceed an individual sorcerer's own Empowerment and require a working circle instead.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium",
				"Great Power"
			],
			"modifiers": [
